WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:00.983
Instructor: In this video,

00:00.983 --> 00:03.930
we'll quickly talk about why you might want to rebase,

00:03.930 --> 00:05.370
we've kind of already touched on it,

00:05.370 --> 00:09.330
but also most importantly, when you should not rebase,

00:09.330 --> 00:11.760
what the golden rule is of rebasing.

00:11.760 --> 00:14.100
So why rebase instead of merge?

00:14.100 --> 00:16.140
Imagine this repo is way more cluttered.

00:16.140 --> 00:18.780
Well, not cluttered, but there's a lot more history,

00:18.780 --> 00:23.760
hundreds of commits and a lot of simultaneous work going on.

00:23.760 --> 00:27.510
We can see with the way I've structured this very clearly

00:27.510 --> 00:30.240
what work I did on the feature branch.

00:30.240 --> 00:33.270
It's these three commits right here because we rebased.

00:33.270 --> 00:35.490
So it doesn't matter if a whole bunch

00:35.490 --> 00:38.580
of, let's say, there's 10 or 50 or 100 other commits

00:38.580 --> 00:41.610
from the master branch and they happen simultaneously

00:41.610 --> 00:42.930
over the course of development

00:42.930 --> 00:44.640
that I was working on feature.

00:44.640 --> 00:46.410
Assuming that I get those changes,

00:46.410 --> 00:47.550
I pull them down to master,

00:47.550 --> 00:49.890
and I rebase onto the master branch,

00:49.890 --> 00:51.510
my feature branch commits

00:51.510 --> 00:53.790
are all located together at the tip.

00:53.790 --> 00:57.270
This makes it easier for someone to review my commits

00:57.270 --> 00:59.220
and take a look at the code base

00:59.220 --> 01:00.960
and try and read what's going on.

01:00.960 --> 01:03.510
In open source projects with thousands of contributors

01:03.510 --> 01:05.760
and tons and tons of work all the time,

01:05.760 --> 01:07.920
this makes it easier for some random person

01:07.920 --> 01:09.210
to go read the history,

01:09.210 --> 01:12.300
go try and understand what did this feature do, right?

01:12.300 --> 01:14.550
I don't have to see like a million merge commits

01:14.550 --> 01:16.260
as part of the commit history.

01:16.260 --> 01:18.150
The end result is the same.

01:18.150 --> 01:20.940
I guess I didn't really actually show you the code here,

01:20.940 --> 01:23.970
but we still end up with the code

01:23.970 --> 01:25.110
coming from the master branch

01:25.110 --> 01:27.090
mixed in with a feature branch.

01:27.090 --> 01:28.620
So we have IMAGE GALLERY ADDED.

01:28.620 --> 01:32.250
It's just the actual history that is updated.

01:32.250 --> 01:34.380
So because we are updating the history,

01:34.380 --> 01:38.850
because rebase rewrites commits, there is a big no-no,

01:38.850 --> 01:40.590
a big thing you should be aware of,

01:40.590 --> 01:42.960
you do not ever wanna rebase commits

01:42.960 --> 01:45.420
that other people already have.

01:45.420 --> 01:49.440
So you don't wanna rebase if you've pushed up some branch

01:49.440 --> 01:51.540
to GitHub or somewhere else

01:51.540 --> 01:55.290
and you collaborators have that work on their machine,

01:55.290 --> 01:56.970
because then they have those commits,

01:56.970 --> 01:58.710
and then all of a sudden you rebase

01:58.710 --> 02:01.650
and you rewrite those commits, you rewrite history,

02:01.650 --> 02:03.870
that is a nightmare, it's not a nightmare,

02:03.870 --> 02:06.652
but it can be really, really annoying to reconcile

02:06.652 --> 02:09.090
when your collaborators have different histories.

02:09.090 --> 02:11.970
You have commits that just don't even exist in their history

02:11.970 --> 02:14.220
and they have commits that don't exist in yours,

02:14.220 --> 02:15.720
and it's all in the past.

02:15.720 --> 02:17.460
That can be very tricky.

02:17.460 --> 02:20.730
So again, the rule, never rebase commits

02:20.730 --> 02:22.500
that you've shared with others.

02:22.500 --> 02:25.800
So what this means is, you wanna be rebasing commits

02:25.800 --> 02:28.410
that you have on your machine and other people don't,

02:28.410 --> 02:31.050
so your feature branches that you're working on.

02:31.050 --> 02:33.600
You don't wanna rebase the master branch

02:33.600 --> 02:35.490
because people, presumably,

02:35.490 --> 02:37.350
have that master branch or the main branch,

02:37.350 --> 02:39.120
they have commits that you have.

02:39.120 --> 02:42.921
So if you rewrite those commits and they're totally new,

02:42.921 --> 02:45.630
that's a problem when you try and push up.

02:45.630 --> 02:47.220
So you don't wanna do that,

02:47.220 --> 02:49.770
at least that's the general principle.

02:49.770 --> 02:51.450
So you work on a feature branch,

02:51.450 --> 02:53.160
whatever you're doing in there, fixing a bug,

02:53.160 --> 02:55.200
adding a new feature, whatever it is,

02:55.200 --> 02:57.960
you can rebase your work from that branch

02:57.960 --> 03:00.930
onto the master branch or onto another branch, right,

03:00.930 --> 03:02.940
you're not changing the master branch.

03:02.940 --> 03:04.230
You don't change the branch

03:04.230 --> 03:06.540
that you are rebasing onto, right?

03:06.540 --> 03:09.210
We didn't change the master branch here.

03:09.210 --> 03:12.240
We only changed my commits on the feature branch.

03:12.240 --> 03:13.800
So that's important.

03:13.800 --> 03:17.100
The branch that you are on when you call rebase

03:17.100 --> 03:20.130
is the one that is rebased, as we talked about.

03:20.130 --> 03:21.273
Let's go back here.

03:22.500 --> 03:24.570
Here I'm on the feature branch.

03:24.570 --> 03:27.000
I rebase onto master, that's fine.

03:27.000 --> 03:28.350
I'm not messing up master,

03:28.350 --> 03:30.420
I'm not changing the history on master.

03:30.420 --> 03:32.910
I'm only changing the history on my feature branch,

03:32.910 --> 03:34.560
I'm changing those commits.

03:34.560 --> 03:35.610
So that's okay.

03:35.610 --> 03:37.800
You just want to avoid rebasing commits,

03:37.800 --> 03:40.080
rewriting history that other people have.

03:40.080 --> 03:42.360
So once you've pushed that feature branch up,

03:42.360 --> 03:45.210
if somebody has pulled that down, if they have that history,

03:45.210 --> 03:46.710
if they're working on it,

03:46.710 --> 03:49.080
eh, you don't wanna rebase.

03:49.080 --> 03:51.420
At least that's a general rule I recommend.

03:51.420 --> 03:55.710
Again, there are ways of fixing this, but it's not fun.

03:55.710 --> 03:59.430
All right, so that is kind of why a lot of people

03:59.430 --> 04:02.460
shy away from rebase or why I was told not to use it

04:02.460 --> 04:05.820
because you can get yourself in situations that are hairy.

04:05.820 --> 04:07.500
If you're collaborating with people,

04:07.500 --> 04:08.520
you're rewriting history.

04:08.520 --> 04:11.460
And if you don't know how to properly use rebase,

04:11.460 --> 04:13.110
that can be a big problem.

04:13.110 --> 04:15.210
So make sure you know what you're doing.

04:15.210 --> 04:16.470
And if you're working at a company,

04:16.470 --> 04:18.090
like they're gonna make it very clear to you

04:18.090 --> 04:19.050
what the workflow is

04:19.050 --> 04:21.390
and if they expect you to rebase everything

04:21.390 --> 04:25.500
or to never base and only merge or rebase in X situations.

04:25.500 --> 04:26.550
It's just part of learning,

04:26.550 --> 04:29.100
you know, the Git workflow at a particular company.

04:29.100 --> 04:31.650
But on your own, if you're working on your own projects,

04:31.650 --> 04:35.790
just you, then you can rebase all the time of course.

04:35.790 --> 04:39.150
But also, even if you're just on a small team,

04:39.150 --> 04:40.050
rebase all the time,

04:40.050 --> 04:42.420
just make sure you know what you are rebasing,

04:42.420 --> 04:44.280
and don't rebase shared history

04:44.280 --> 04:46.290
any commits people already have.

04:46.290 --> 04:49.380
Only rebase stuff that is yours and yours alone.

04:49.380 --> 04:51.990
Rebase it and then you can push it up, right?

04:51.990 --> 04:53.970
You get this nice clean history.

04:53.970 --> 04:55.890
You don't have all those merge commits.

04:55.890 --> 04:57.810
Do that, and then you can share that.

04:57.810 --> 05:00.060
So I'll show another example of this next

05:00.060 --> 05:02.370
with some conflicts if you wanna see how that works.

05:02.370 --> 05:04.470
It's the same principle roughly

05:04.470 --> 05:07.500
as resolving merge conflicts when you're merging,

05:07.500 --> 05:08.763
but I'll demo it anyway.
